  <h1>The Cost of Observability — When the Measurement Is the Hot Path</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: adding "one cheap top-of-book read" per event turned this repo's
  31-nanosecond book update into a 72-<em>micro</em>second one — a 2,000× slowdown that the docs had
  even predicted. This lesson is how to find, prove, and reason about that class of cost.</p>
</header>

<h2>1 · The concept</h2>
<p>Every signal, metric, or log you attach to a hot path is itself code with a cost, and that cost
obeys different math than the path it observes. Three rules capture most real cases:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Frequency multiplies everything.</strong> A read that's free when called occasionally is
  the whole budget when called per event. The flat book's <code>top_of_book()</code> scan was designed
  for "query rarely"; the signal engine calls it 1.5 million times a day.</li>
  <li><strong>Complexity constants hide in domain parameters.</strong> "O(range) linear sweep over a
  small array" was honest at 30,001 ticks (a $3 window). Nobody re-read that sentence when the range
  became 200,000 ticks ($20). Same code, same big-O, 6.7× the constant.</li>
  <li><strong>Prove the mechanism by varying its parameter.</strong> Suspecting the scan is a
  hypothesis. Running the same day with two different price ranges and watching cost scale with the
  ratio is a confirmed model: cost ratio 1.47 vs range ratio 1.48 ⇒ the scan is ~100% of the cost.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2 · Minimal standalone example</h2>
<pre><code><span class="com"># The observer's complexity, not the system's, dominates:</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> event <span class="kw">in</span> feed:              <span class="com"># N events</span>
    book.apply(event)            <span class="com"># O(1)   — 31 ns measured</span>
    snapshot = book.top()        <span class="com"># O(R)   — R = declared tick range!</span>
    signals.append(f(snapshot))  <span class="com"># O(1)</span>
<span class="com"># Total: O(N·R). N = 1.5e6, R = 2e5  →  3e11 slot visits ≈ your 110 seconds.</span>

<span class="com"># The experiment that converts suspicion into proof:</span>
<span class="com">#   run(R=200_000)  →  72,568 ns/event</span>
<span class="com">#   run(R=135_000)  →  49,212 ns/event</span>
<span class="com">#   cost ratio 1.47  ≈  range ratio 1.48  →  the O(R) term is everything.</span></code></pre>

<h2>3 · In this project</h2>
<p><span class="path">docs/perf/02-microstructure-signals.md</span> — the full write-up. Headline table:
bare book 30.9 ns/message (synthetic bench) vs book+engine on the real AAPL day: 72,568 ns/event at
200k ticks, 49,212 ns/event at 135k ticks. The wall-clock was 110 seconds for one symbol-day — fine
for tonight's research, hopeless for a 500-symbol backtest, which is why the write-up specifies the
follow-up: incremental best-index tracking in the book (O(1) reads, short repair scans), projected
~100× improvement, deferred to its own before/after write-up.</p>
<p><span class="path">cpp/itch/microstructure_signals.hpp</span> — <code>emit()</code> is the per-event
observer: <code>top_of_book()</code> + OFI arithmetic + a vector push. The arithmetic is nanoseconds;
the scan (<span class="path">cpp/itch/order_book_flat.hpp:86-105</span>) is everything else.</p>
<p><span class="path">src/oil_alpha/microstructure/plots.py</span> — the same discipline applied to
<em>rendering</em>: 1.5M plotly points would hang the browser, so <code>_decimate()</code> uniformly
thins to ≤5,000 rows. Uniform decimation preserves session-scale shape (trends, regimes) and
deliberately sacrifices single-event spikes — a documented trade-off, not an accident.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Contrast with docs/perf/01:</strong> lesson 0002's flat-book story was
"the right data structure made the book 2× faster." This chapter is its sequel and inversion: the
same structure's convenience read, moved into a per-event loop with a 6.7× bigger domain parameter,
became a 2,000× tax. Data structures aren't fast or slow — <em>access patterns</em> are.</div>

<h2>4 · Often confused: throughput cost vs latency cost</h2>
<div class="scrollbox"><table>
  <tr><th></th><th>Batch/throughput view (this lesson)</th><th>Tail-latency view (lesson 0002, perf/01)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Question</td><td>How long for 1.5M events? (wall clock, mean ns/event)</td><td>How late is the slowest 1-in-1000 event? (p99, p99.9)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Killer</td><td>Per-event work × event count (O(N·R))</td><td>Rare stalls: cache misses, allocation, OS jitter</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Fix shape</td><td>Reduce per-event complexity (incremental tracking)</td><td>Reduce variance (locality, no allocation in path)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>When each matters</td><td>Research/backtests over big files</td><td>Live trading, fleeting opportunities</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Also confused:</td><td colspan="2"><strong>Decimation vs aggregation</strong> — decimation (every k-th
  point) preserves shape cheaply but drops extremes; aggregation (per-bucket min/max/OHLC) keeps
  extremes at more cost. The dashboard decimates; the λ estimator aggregates (1s bars). Each choice
  matches what its consumer needs.</td></tr>
</table></div>

